# Target deployment
buildkitd.toml

# NDK
local.properties

# Wayfinder specifics
var/conf/wayfinder0.state

# A containerised node's mesh identity, membership certificate and trust
# anchor, for a deployment that boots them from a static `auth:` block rather
# than enrolling over the management API the way the compose stack does. The
# seed *is* the node's identity on the mesh; it is minted per deployment and
# belongs wherever that host's secrets live, never in this repo.
containers/secrets/

# Static site build output (scripts/build-site.sh)
dist/

# Cloudflare
.wrangler

# The HIL board inventory: probe and USB serial numbers are specific to one
# bench, so each machine keeps its own. `example.hil.toml` is the template.
hil.toml

# One bench's own deployment, in three parts. Same reasoning as `hil.toml`
# above: these name hardware that exists on exactly one machine — an nRF52840
# dongle, and the USB-path-derived NIC name it presents on the port it happens
# to occupy — so they belong beside the machine, not in the repo. The committed
# docker-compose.vpn.yml and containers/node-vpn.yml stay portable, and nothing
# in git names this host's NIC.
#
#   * the launch script, which also performs the one setup step no compose file
#     can express (moving that NIC into the tailscale container's network
#     namespace);
#   * a compose layer stacked on docker-compose.vpn.yml; and
#   * the node config that layer mounts.
#
# `<host>.yml` rather than a fixed name so a second bench can keep its own.
scripts/local-*.sh
docker-compose.gantz.yml
containers/gantz.yml

# Transit files for scripts/local-enroll-dongle.sh: a CSR and the certificate
# the CA issues against it. Not secret (a CSR carries only public keys, and a
# membership certificate is presented on every connection), but per-bench and
# regenerable from the board at any time.
.local-enroll/

# Pass markers GitHub Actions restores into the checkout (scripts/ci-cached.sh).
.ci-passed/
